 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134822 #edb/ref 1061 32553 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134822 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5d2f80d4-10e2-5fdc-b52f-7b5cadac99d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134822 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133876 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134826 #edb/ref 1061 33322 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134826 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "eb39b82a-9c43-56ff-9c10-70ad9f735fab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134826 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133880 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134842 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134848 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134842 #edb/ref 1061 34359 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134842 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6a9298c4-106a-569c-bd0b-5d24d3e71b0f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134842 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135114 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134844 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134842 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134844 #edb/ref 1061 35525 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134844 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "77b5a013-eab6-50c3-9f63-2a51f2e967b2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134844 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133889 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134848 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134848 #edb/ref 1061 35536 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134848 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "88b27d23-7358-51e8-9457-a8b063ed5a52" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134848 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135120 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134849 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134822 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134849 #edb/ref 1061 35537 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134849 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e856caa2-317e-5779-b004-597af558a506" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134849 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135121 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135102 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that $\\mathbf F$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2,$ expressed in terms of its component functions as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y) = P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf i + Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nIf the first partial derivatives of $P$ and $Q$ exist, then the **divergence** of $\\mathbf F,$ denoted $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F,$ is the scalar given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F = \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}.\n$$\n\nNotice that if we define the differential operator $\\nabla$ (a.k.a. the \"del\" operator) as \n\n$$\n\\nabla =  \\mathbf i\\,\\dfrac{\\partial }{\\partial x} +  \\mathbf j\\,\\dfrac{\\partial }{\\partial y},\n$$\n\n \n\nthen the divergence of $\\mathbf F$ is given by the dot product\n\n$$\n\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F = \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F.\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose that the vector field $\\mathbf F$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y) = 2xy\\, \\mathbf i + e^{yx} \\, \\mathbf j .\n$$\n\nThen, we can calculate $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F}$ using the dot product formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =∇\\cdot F \\\\  & =(i\\,\\frac{∂}{∂x}+j\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y})\\cdot (2xy\\,i+{e}^{yx}\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{yx}) \\\\  & =2y+x{e}^{yx}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135102 #edb/ref 1065 5116 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135102 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "086f12c3-0ab5-54ed-8b4e-5c70e8c7c7e5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135102 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135114 #edb/ref 1064 "Our goal now is to develop an intuitive understanding of what the divergence of a vector field measures.\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y)$ and the point $S,$ shown below. Imagine that this vector field represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. \n\n![](images/a7/a785c84976242d699af11ab2203600e2007e95e35b60665ba9882c8f0f25bce3.png)\n\n \n\nWe can see from our diagram that $S$ acts like a **source**. You can think of this as an open faucet, pushing water onto the plane and away from itself. \n\nSince $S$ acts like a source, $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F$ is nonzero. By convention, if a point $S$ acts like a source, the divergence is positive:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F > 0\n$$\n\nIn this particular case, *every* point in our vector field acts as a source. So $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F > 0$ everywhere.\n\nIf we reverse the situation, we get the following:\n\n![](images/b4/b4c923bbde36568e4e2f36f7d388622a520d0799d558049f72d0194e0bd679f7.png)\n\n \n\nWe can see from our diagram that $T$ acts like a **sink**. You can think of this as an open plughole that sucks in the surrounding water.\n\nSince $T$ acts like a sink, $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F$ is also nonzero. By convention, if a point $T$ acts like a sink, the divergence is negative:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F < 0\n$$\n\nEvery point in our vector field acts like a sink in this case. So $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F < 0$ everywhere.\n\nFinally, note that if $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F = 0$ everywhere in the domain of some vector field $\\mathbf F,$ we say that $\\mathbf F$ is **solenoidal.**" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135114 #edb/ref 1065 5518 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135114 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a2cdf73b-116c-5cf4-a5a2-a8c95e447127" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135114 #edb/ref 1067 "Physical Interpretation of Divergence" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135120 #edb/ref 1064 "The following two vector fields behave either as a source or a sink. Therefore, they both have divergence.\n\n![](images/93/939efa85ebfe4805a27595b60a797b8a3435075590ee77f7a18d04bd76d91f1a.png)\n\nThe following vector fields are solenoidal (i.e., they each have zero divergence).\n\n![](images/ed/edb2f908fa78439bf985b5754c9fcc5968c21926dbe124297b67af046a9f70a3.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135120 #edb/ref 1065 5793 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135120 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ddbe7ae2-caaf-5436-b1c7-aabc0eec115d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135120 #edb/ref 1067 "Examples of Vector Field With Divergence and Solenoidal Vector Fields" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135121 #edb/ref 1064 "There are a few things we should note about the divergence:\n\n- We can only compute the divergence of a *vector* field, and a vector field's divergence is a *scalar* field. So if $G(x,y) = \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F,$ then we have the following map:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\,\\,\\,\\mathbf F(x,y)\\,\\,\\,}_{\\textrm{vector field}} \\mapsto\\underbrace{\\,\\,\\,G(x,y)\\,\\,\\,}_{\\textrm{scalar field}}\n$$\n\nIn contrast, if $f(x,y)$ is a scalar field, then the statement $\\textrm{div}\\, f$ has no meaning.\n\n- We can easily extend the notion of divergence to three dimensions. So, if $\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j + R\\,\\mathbf k,$ then $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F = \\nabla\\cdot \\mathbf F = \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} + \\dfrac{\\partial R}{\\partial z},\n$$\n\nwhere the three-dimensional differential operator $\\nabla$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\nabla =  \\mathbf i\\,\\dfrac{\\partial }{\\partial x} +  \\mathbf j\\,\\dfrac{\\partial }{\\partial y} + \\mathbf k\\,\\dfrac{\\partial }{\\partial z}.\n$$\n\n- The divergence of a vector field at a point $P$ gives us helpful information about the tendency of the vector field to behave like a source or a sink at $P.$ We'll describe this in more detail shortly." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135121 #edb/ref 1065 5794 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135121 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "9b8de083-51c2-581b-ad36-a773dca8af05" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135121 #edb/ref 1067 "Some Important Notes About the Divergence of a Vector Field" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186257781 #edb/ref 1080 "144" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186257781 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186257781 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "71cbe9b9-abfb-59ee-9a3b-f7f689c26688" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186257981 #edb/ref 1080 "76" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186257981 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186257981 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "88b7f1be-ad85-5371-a6af-84faa05bd3aa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186258609 #edb/ref 1080 "48" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186258609 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186258609 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d1c60dec-38f0-52c7-b0aa-6a4369234d40" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186258639 #edb/ref 1080 "96" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186258639 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186258639 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d40a47e0-a307-5498-8db8-91105ba44fa3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186258838 #edb/ref 1080 "84" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186258838 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186258838 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "edb9593d-e1cc-5ea7-ae77-b57065d0d67e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259120 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186257781 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259120 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186257981 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259120 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186258609 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259120 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186258639 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259120 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186258838 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259120 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259120 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259120 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186258639 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259120 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "31c01b0b-506b-5b5b-95a1-7bc8278302dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259490 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259120 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259490 #edb/ref 1069 "q-123200" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259490 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259490 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector field $F=(x-y)^{3}\\,i+(y+z)^{3}\\,j+(x+z)^{3}\\,k.$ Evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(-2,2,2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259490 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "74e7bf88-8da6-528c-84d5-ded22deb1ee4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259490 #edb/ref 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x-y)^{3})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}((y+z)^{3})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}((x+z)^{3}) \\\\  & =3(x-y)^{2}+3(y+z)^{2}+3(x+z)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(-2,2,2),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F(-2,2,2) & =3((-2)-(2))^{2}+3((2)+(2))^{2}+3((-2)+(2))^{2} \\\\  & =3(-4)^{2}+3(4)^{2}+3(0)^{2} \\\\  & =96.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263155 #edb/ref 1080 "xy{z}^{2}+xz" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263155 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263155 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "10bcdd62-0b56-5da2-a50a-586faa6696b0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263355 #edb/ref 1080 "y{z}^{2}+xy{e}^{y}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263355 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263355 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "295ca0f4-57e7-5a42-ac1c-c9565fd958a3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263371 #edb/ref 1080 "5y{z}^{2}+x{e}^{y}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263371 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263371 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2be15acb-dbc4-5b00-98a8-38e26089e1ec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263576 #edb/ref 1080 "y{z}^{2}+xy+y" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263576 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263576 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "40fe9bd6-26ef-51ba-a8ee-4aa3a8d37085" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265240 #edb/ref 1080 "x+{e}^{y}+{z}^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265240 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 1759